From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alex Chaplinsky <ai@joineve.ai>
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] balloon: give freed guest memory back to macOS

On macOS, free page reporting calls madvise(MADV_FREE) on each reported
range. That doesn't lower the VMM's footprint (phys_footprint, which
Activity Monitor shows and memory pressure is based on): a guest that
allocates and frees 1 GiB keeps costing the host 1 GiB until it exits.

MADV_FREE_REUSABLE drops the footprint at once in a plain process, but
not here: memory that is mapped into the guest with hv_vm_map() stays
charged for as long as that mapping exists, whatever madvise() says.

So unmap the reported range from the guest, mark it reusable, and map it
back. The guest leaves reported pages alone until the report is
acknowledged, so it never sees the gap. When it touches them again, the
stage-2 fault brings them back and Darwin charges them to the footprint
again. Darwin documents MADV_FREE_REUSE as the call to make before
reusing such pages; free page reporting gives no reuse signal, but
because the first touch after the remap always faults, the accounting
comes back without it.

A 2 GiB guest allocates 1 GiB, frees it, allocates 1 GiB again filled
with a pattern and checks it. VMM footprint (macOS 27, Apple M-series):

                      allocated   freed    reallocated   contents
  MADV_FREE (before)    1150 MB   1150 MB      1735 MB   intact
  MADV_FREE_REUSABLE    1146 MB   1146 MB      1727 MB   intact
  this patch            1148 MB    149 MB      1150 MB   intact

The footprint after reallocating matches what the guest uses, so it is
not under-reported, and it keeps tracking over repeated cycles. Linux is
unchanged (MADV_DONTNEED).

If the range can't be unmapped, it is left mapped, which only means the
memory isn't returned. If it can't be mapped back, the VMM aborts rather
than acknowledge the report: the guest would later touch a range nothing
can service.
---
diff --git a/src/devices/src/virtio/balloon/device.rs b/src/devices/src/virtio/balloon/device.rs
index 85b2812..fbf1ecb 100644
--- a/src/devices/src/virtio/balloon/device.rs
+++ b/src/devices/src/virtio/balloon/device.rs
@@ -93,17 +93,7 @@ impl Balloon {
                     "balloon: should release guest_addr={:?} host_addr={:p} len={}",
                     desc.addr, host_addr, desc.len
                 );
-                #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
-                let advice = libc::MADV_DONTNEED;
-                #[cfg(target_os = "macos")]
-                let advice = libc::MADV_FREE;
-                unsafe {
-                    libc::madvise(
-                        host_addr as *mut libc::c_void,
-                        desc.len.try_into().unwrap(),
-                        advice,
-                    )
-                };
+                release_memory(desc.addr.0, host_addr, desc.len.try_into().unwrap());
             }
 
             have_used = true;
@@ -116,6 +106,65 @@ impl Balloon {
     }
 }
 
+/// Return the host memory behind a free page report to the OS.
+#[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
+fn release_memory(_guest_addr: u64, host_addr: *mut u8, len: usize) {
+    unsafe { libc::madvise(host_addr as *mut libc::c_void, len, libc::MADV_DONTNEED) };
+}
+
+/// Return the host memory behind a free page report to the OS.
+///
+/// On macOS, memory mapped into the guest stays charged to this process's
+/// footprint whatever madvise() says: neither MADV_FREE nor MADV_FREE_REUSABLE
+/// changes it while the guest mapping exists. So unmap the range from the
+/// guest, mark it reusable, which drops it from the footprint at once, and map
+/// it back. The guest's next touch faults the pages back in and charges them
+/// again, so the footprint follows what the guest uses even though nothing
+/// calls MADV_FREE_REUSE (the guest doesn't say when it reuses a page).
+///
+/// The guest doesn't touch reported pages until the report is acknowledged,
+/// so the range being briefly unmapped is invisible to it. If the range can't
+/// be unmapped, it stays mapped and charged, which is safe. If it can't be
+/// mapped back, the VM is stopped: the guest would otherwise touch a hole.
+#[cfg(target_os = "macos")]
+fn release_memory(guest_addr: u64, host_addr: *mut u8, len: usize) {
+    use hvf::bindings::{
+        hv_vm_map, hv_vm_unmap, HV_MEMORY_EXEC, HV_MEMORY_READ, HV_MEMORY_WRITE, HV_SUCCESS,
+    };
+
+    // Hypervisor.framework maps whole host pages.
+    let page_size = unsafe { libc::sysconf(libc::_SC_PAGESIZE) } as u64;
+    if (guest_addr | host_addr as u64 | len as u64) % page_size != 0 {
+        debug!("balloon: unaligned range at {guest_addr:#x}, not released");
+        return;
+    }
+
+    unsafe {
+        if hv_vm_unmap(guest_addr, len) != HV_SUCCESS {
+            error!("balloon: failed to unmap guest memory at {guest_addr:#x}");
+            return;
+        }
+        libc::madvise(
+            host_addr as *mut libc::c_void,
+            len,
+            libc::MADV_FREE_REUSABLE,
+        );
+        let flags = HV_MEMORY_READ | HV_MEMORY_WRITE | HV_MEMORY_EXEC;
+        if hv_vm_map(
+            host_addr as *mut libc::c_void,
+            guest_addr,
+            len,
+            flags.into(),
+        ) != HV_SUCCESS
+        {
+            // The guest would fault on this range the next time it uses it, and nothing could
+            // service that fault. Stopping the VM is the only safe outcome.
+            error!("balloon: failed to map back guest memory at {guest_addr:#x}; stopping the VM");
+            std::process::abort();
+        }
+    }
+}
+
